---
title: Clinical conversation agents and retrieval
org: Sirka
period: 2025–2026
role: I built the agent service end to end as its sole author — retrieval, agents, guardrails, the evaluation suite and the deployment pipeline. The surrounding CRM it plugs into was built by the wider team.
---

Sirka runs a telehealth weight-management clinic in Indonesia. Patient and lead conversations
arrive over WhatsApp and Instagram, and every one of them was answered by a person. The goal was
to lower that load and answer outside working hours, with booking a consultation as the thing
success was measured by.

This is private company code, so what follows describes the design and the measured results
without links or excerpts. No prompts, clinical rules, prices or knowledge-base content appear
here.

## Shape of the system

Two tool-calling agents — one facing new leads, one handling post-consultation follow-up — plus
a third that pre-fills a medical record from the consultation transcript. Each runs as a
tool-calling loop against a hosted model, with tools that call back into the CRM over an
authenticated internal boundary. The agents never hold credentials for the downstream clinical
systems; they ask the CRM, and the CRM makes those calls.

There is no agent framework here, which was a deliberate choice rather than an aesthetic one.
The prompts carry medical safety wording that clinicians needed to review and amend directly,
and a framework that assembles prompts at runtime puts that text one layer away from the people
responsible for it. I wrote the loop instead. The cost is that I own the retry and tool-dispatch
logic; the benefit is that every word sent to the model is visible in one place, and every
prompt section, guard pattern and classifier is an operations-editable setting with a validation
check that refuses an invalid save.

## Retrieval: three signals, and why the third exists

The knowledge base is chunked deterministically along its own section anchors, so a chunk
boundary never moves unexpectedly between ingests. Internal-only sections are excluded at
ingest. Price and promotional sections are flagged volatile, and only collections marked
authoritative are allowed to supply a price at all. Blog content is versioned by namespace so a
bad ingest can be rolled back rather than repaired in place.

Search fuses three rankings with reciprocal rank fusion:

- **Dense** — cosine similarity over vector embeddings, stored as half-precision vectors with an
  HNSW index.
- **Lexical** — full-text ranking in the database.
- **Code overlap** — exact matching on clinical and product codes.

The third signal is there for a concrete reason. The text tokeniser normalised a product code
and its suffixed variant to the same token, which made two genuinely different products
indistinguishable to lexical search. Rather than fight the tokeniser, I indexed the codes
separately and gave exact matches their own ranking to contribute to the fusion.

Embeddings use a model that supports Matryoshka truncation, cut from 4096 to 1024 dimensions for
storage and speed. I did not take that on faith — there is a script that checks 1024-dimension
rankings against the full-width rankings, and the truncation was adopted only after it agreed.
Query embeddings are cached, because embedding turned out to account for roughly as much latency
as the retrieval itself.

## What the retrieval actually scores

An 81-case evaluation set, spanning ten categories — prohibited requests, FAQ, pricing, blog
content, booking, product questions, multi-turn, out-of-scope and typos — at k = 8:

<div class="table-wrap">

| Mode | recall@8 | MRR |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Dense | 0.941 | 0.862 |
| Lexical | 0.309 | 0.270 |
| **Hybrid** | **0.971** | 0.860 |

</div>

Hybrid wins on recall and is level on MRR. Indonesian and English scored within a point of each
other, which mattered because the clinic's conversations mix both.

The number I would want a reviewer to ask about is multi-turn recall@8, which came in at
**0.667** — materially worse than the rest, because a follow-up question that depends on earlier
context retrieves poorly when embedded on its own. It is written down as the next thing to fix
rather than folded into an average.

## Safety as code, not as a judge

The temptation with guardrails is to ask a model to check the model. I rejected that for the
safety gate, for two reasons that I think generalise.

First: a guardrail that depends on a ranking function is not a guardrail. If a safety
instruction is a retrieved chunk, then its presence depends on retrieval quality, and a
guardrail that is usually present is not one. So the safety context is pinned into every request
regardless of its retrieval rank.

Second: a flaky gate gets skipped. A probabilistic check in CI that fails occasionally for
reasons nobody can reproduce teaches a team to re-run it, and a gate everyone re-runs is not a
gate. So the blocking checks are deterministic functions:

- Emergency-phrase detection, which runs **before** the model is called, not after.
- Dose-figure detection, so the agent cannot state a quantity.
- Personal-medical-question detection, routing to a clinician.
- An internal-terminology blocklist.
- A price-citation check, so a price can only come from an authoritative source.
- A grounding check that refuses an uncited claim.
- Formality and message-length limits, which are product requirements rather than safety ones.

Model-based classifiers still exist, for urgency, booking intent, lab-result reading, opt-out
and similar — but each is documented as fail-open or fail-closed according to what failure
costs. The urgency classifier is the clearest case: it can only *add* an emergency flag, never
clear one. That constraint came out of testing, where a small model failed to flag an
unambiguous chest-pain phrasing in Indonesian. The model is allowed to escalate and not to
de-escalate.

A lesson from the first end-to-end safety run is worth recording. It reported four failures. All
four turned out to be faulty assertions — the checker was blind to negation and was reading
correct refusals as violations. After fixing the checker, all twelve cases passed. The failures
were in the test, not the system, which is a good argument for reading every failure before
acting on the aggregate.

## Order safety

The post-consultation agent can place an order, which is the highest-risk thing in the system.
It is checked twice, independently, in the agent and again in the backend: only drugs on the
prescribing record, never more than the prescribed quantity, one order per record. Plan changes
go to the consulting doctor for approval rather than being applied.

Delivery phone numbers are parsed from the patient's own message rather than taken from the
model's output, because on one occasion the model produced a plausible invented number. And one
category of medication cannot be shipped outside the metropolitan area — that check fails
closed, routing to a human when the delivery region cannot be established.

## Operations and MLOps

- **Tests:** around 370 test functions. The safety and unit gate runs in CI *before* the
  container build and before cloud authentication, so a safety failure costs nothing downstream
  and cannot be bypassed by a successful build.
- **Nightly end-to-end run** against the live model, separate from the deterministic suite,
  because the deterministic gate deliberately does not exercise model behaviour.
- **Audit trail:** every turn writes a compliance record. It stores setting *names*, never
  patient values — the record proves which configuration produced a reply without becoming a
  second copy of patient data.
- **Per-turn traces** surfaced to agents in the inbox, so a human taking over a conversation can
  see what the model did and which tools it called.
- **Provider pinning**, after measuring roughly 1 second to first token when pinned against
  about 14.7 seconds unpinned on the same model.
- Model routing with documented fallbacks per agent, chosen on cost, latency and healthcare
  benchmark performance rather than on a single leaderboard.
- Containerised, deployed to staging on pull request and to production on release, with
  path-filtered workflows so each service builds independently.

The handover documentation records known gaps as well, including the fact that provider-side
traces cannot be joined to a conversation ID. I would rather hand over a list of what does not
work than let someone discover it.

## Stack

Python, FastAPI, PostgreSQL with pgvector, hosted models through OpenRouter, Docker, GitHub
Actions, Google Cloud.
